e need to use the property that a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has exactly one solution if and only if its discriminant is zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation \\( 9x^2 + nx + 36 = 0 \\), we identify the coefficients as \\( a = 9 \\), \\( b = n \\), and \\( c = 36 \\). The discriminant of this equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = n^2 - 4 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 36\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the discriminant, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = n^2 - 4 \\cdot 324 = n^2 - 1296\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\n\\[\nn^2 - 1296 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\), we add 1296 to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nn^2 = 1296\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nn = \\pm \\sqrt{1296} = \\pm 36\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the positive value of \\( n \\). Therefore, the positive value of \\( n \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{36}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:156"}
